---
import { links } from "../content/site";
import FeatureTile from "../ui/FeatureTile.astro";
import SectionHead from "../ui/SectionHead.astro";
import ChatView from "../views/chat/ChatView.astro";
import McpView from "../views/mcp/McpView.astro";
import PromptsView from "../views/prompts/PromptsView.astro";
import WorktreeView from "../views/worktree/WorktreeView.astro";
import "./Features.css";
---

<section id="features" class="section features" aria-labelledby="features-title">
  <div class="wrap-wide">
    <SectionHead eyebrow="Features" id="features-title">
      <Fragment slot="title"
        >Plan in a chat, control every prompt, and test in the worktree.</Fragment
      >
      <p>
        These tools use the same local task store as the board. A task that an agent creates in a
        chat or through MCP shows on the board at once.
      </p>
    </SectionHead>
    <div class="features-grid">
      <FeatureTile
        id="feature-chat"
        label="Chats"
        title="Plan a feature in a chat, and get tasks on the board."
        size="full"
      >
        A chat works outside a task. Give it a custom role and a reusable prompt. The agent can
        search your tasks and create new ones.
        <ChatView slot="view" />
      </FeatureTile>
      <FeatureTile
        id="feature-mcp"
        label="MCP"
        title="Use your tasks from any MCP client."
        size="half"
      >
        Add <a class="text-link" href={links.mcp}><code>@openducktor/mcp</code></a> to another agent.
        It can search, read, create, and update your tasks. It sends each call to the running app, so
        the board shows a new task at once.
        <McpView slot="view" />
      </FeatureTile>
      <FeatureTile
        id="feature-prompts"
        label="Prompts"
        title="Read and replace every built-in prompt."
        size="half"
      >
        The settings show each built-in prompt that OpenDucktor sends to an agent. Replace a prompt
        for all repositories or for one repository. OpenDucktor checks the placeholders of each
        active override, and it does not save until they are valid.
        <PromptsView slot="view" />
      </FeatureTile>
      <FeatureTile
        id="feature-worktree"
        label="Worktrees"
        title="Try each change in its own worktree."
        size="full"
      >
        The Builder works in a Git worktree for the task. Start the dev servers of the repository in
        that worktree, read their logs, and open the worktree in your editor.
        <WorktreeView slot="view" />
      </FeatureTile>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
